A stack of strings gives a direct iterative solution in Go. Every operand starts a small prefix expression. An operator joins the two latest expressions with itself at the front.

The right expression must be popped before the left one. For `xy-`, that produces `-xy`, not `-yx`.

```go
func postToPre(postfix string) string {
    stack := make([]string, 0, len(postfix))

    isOperator := func(ch byte) bool {
        return ch == '+' || ch == '-' || ch == '*' || ch == '/' || ch == '^'
    }

    for i := 0; i < len(postfix); i++ {
        ch := postfix[i]
        if ch == ' ' {
            continue
        }

        if !isOperator(ch) {
            stack = append(stack, string(ch))
            continue
        }

        right := stack[len(stack)-1]
        stack = stack[:len(stack)-1]
        left := stack[len(stack)-1]
        stack = stack[:len(stack)-1]

        stack = append(stack, string(ch)+left+right)
    }

    if len(stack) == 0 {
        return ""
    }
    return stack[len(stack)-1]
}
```

Dry run with `ab/c+`: `a` and `b` form `/ab`, then `c` is pushed, and `+` creates `+/abc`.

The code skips spaces and supports the usual binary operators. It expects single-character operands, which is the common format for this problem.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and stored result strings.

A small stack is enough here. Read the postfix expression from left to right. Operands are pushed as they are found. When an operator appears, remove the right operand first, then the left operand, and place the operator before both parts.

For `ab+c*`, the final stack item becomes `*+abc`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    bool isOperator(char ch) {
        return ch == '+' || ch == '-' || ch == '*' || ch == '/' || ch == '^';
    }

public:
    string postToPre(string postfix) {
        vector<string> stack;

        for (char ch : postfix) {
            if (ch == ' ') {
                continue;
            }

            if (!isOperator(ch)) {
                stack.push_back(string(1, ch));
            } else {
                string right = stack.back();
                stack.pop_back();
                string left = stack.back();
                stack.pop_back();

                stack.push_back(string(1, ch) + left + right);
            }
        }

        return stack.empty() ? "" : stack.back();
    }
};
```

Dry run for `ab+c*`:
- Push `a`, then `b`
- `+` combines them into `+ab`
- Push `c`
- `*` combines `+ab` and `c` into `*+abc`

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and built expressions.

This one first turns the input into tokens. That makes it usable with space-separated operands such as `12 3 + 4 *`, instead of treating every character as a separate operand.

The stack stores complete prefix pieces. For each operator, the last popped item is the right token, so the order is important.

```python
class Solution:
    def postToPre(self, postfix: str) -> str:
        if not postfix.strip():
            return ""

        if any(char.isspace() for char in postfix):
            tokens = postfix.split()
        else:
            tokens = list(postfix)

        operators = {"+", "-", "*", "/", "^"}
        stack = []

        for token in tokens:
            if token not in operators:
                stack.append(token)
            else:
                right = stack.pop()
                left = stack.pop()
                stack.append(token + left + right)

        return stack[-1] if stack else ""
```

The no-space form is read one character at a time, while whitespace separates larger operands. For example, `12 3 + 4 *` becomes `*+1234`.

The input should describe one valid postfix expression. If operands contain multiple characters without spaces, there is no way to know where one operand ends.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, where `n` is the input length.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for tokens and stack entries.

Instead of storing the whole prefix text at every operator, this version stores small expression nodes. Each operator node keeps its left and right children. A preorder walk then prints the operator before its children, which is exactly prefix order.

This also keeps the operand order clear because the first item removed is the right child.

```java
class Solution {
    private static class Node {
        char value;
        Node left;
        Node right;

        Node(char value) {
            this.value = value;
        }

        Node(char value, Node left, Node right) {
            this.value = value;
            this.left = left;
            this.right = right;
        }
    }

    private boolean isOperator(char ch) {
        return ch == '+' || ch == '-' || ch == '*' || ch == '/' || ch == '^';
    }

    private void buildPrefix(Node node, StringBuilder result) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        result.append(node.value);
        buildPrefix(node.left, result);
        buildPrefix(node.right, result);
    }

    public String postToPre(String postfix) {
        java.util.ArrayDeque<Node> stack = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < postfix.length(); i++) {
            char ch = postfix.charAt(i);
            if (ch == ' ') {
                continue;
            }

            if (!isOperator(ch)) {
                stack.push(new Node(ch));
            } else {
                Node right = stack.pop();
                Node left = stack.pop();
                stack.push(new Node(ch, left, right));
            }
        }

        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            return "";
        }

        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
        buildPrefix(stack.peek(), result);
        return result.toString();
    }
}
```

An empty input returns an empty string. The method also ignores spaces, so inputs such as `a b +` work for single-character operands.

Time complexity: `O(n)` for building the tree and visiting its nodes.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the expression tree and stack.
